1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:00,906
Hey everyone!

2
00:00:00,906 --> 00:00:07,802
Welcome to the 25-hour milestone for Hux on Hack Club's Half Life, covering our sprint from hour 15 to 25!

3
00:00:07,802 --> 00:00:12,239
With the schematic locked, I routed the entire 2-layer PCB in KiCad.

4
00:00:12,239 --> 00:00:20,249
I ran the 40MHz display SPI bus on top, poured solid ground planes on both sides, and added stitching vias to kill RF noise.

5
00:00:20,249 --> 00:00:27,427
After passing DRC with zero errors, I exported the JLCPCB Gerbers and automated SMT pick-and-place files.

6
00:00:27,427 --> 00:00:39,031
Next, I wrote the firmware in C++: an ST7789 display driver running a 60fps procedural vector eye engine with real-time gaze and blinking.

7
00:00:39,031 --> 00:00:46,479
I configured dual DMA channels for the I2S mic and amp, plus an RMT driver for the eight Arc-Reactor NeoPixels.

8
00:00:46,479 --> 00:00:53,593
Connected to my local Home Assistant over WebSockets, command latency is under 15 milliseconds at 62 milliamps idle.

9
00:00:53,593 --> 00:00:56,854
Hux is fully routed, coded, and ready for fabrication.

10
00:00:56,854 --> 00:00:58,291
See you at the next milestone!

